1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
The gadgets in James Bond films are the best the movie world has to offer.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
The difference between men and boys is the price of the toys.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:13,000
And once you've worked on a Bond, you've worked with the big boys.

4
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:19,000
They push the limits of technological wizardry and stunt work like no one else.

5
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Forty divers are in the water fighting each other.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:24,000
I don't think that's ever been equaled.

7
00:00:24,001 --> 00:00:28,000
It's just a mass of humanity and machinery churning around in the ocean.

8
00:00:28,001 --> 00:00:34,000
When the lights go down in the theater, audiences always know they're about to see movie-making magic,

9
00:00:34,001 --> 00:00:38,000
thanks to the gadgets of James Bond.

10
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:53,000
In the world of creating gadgets for the movies,

11
00:00:53,001 --> 00:00:58,000
one name sets the standard for delivering a combination of speed,

12
00:00:58,001 --> 00:01:01,000
cleverness, and cinema magic.

13
00:01:01,001 --> 00:01:03,000
James Bond.

14
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:09,000
In more than 40 years of Bond films, the gadgets used by 007

15
00:01:09,001 --> 00:01:14,000
have become as recognizable as the phrase, shake, not stir.

16
00:01:14,001 --> 00:01:18,000
Bond was sort of a Hugh Hefner sort of character

17
00:01:18,001 --> 00:01:22,000
who possessed everything the average male would want.

18
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:25,480
male would want, all the women at his disposal,

19
00:01:25,481 --> 00:01:28,240
unlimited expense accounts, and of course,

20
00:01:28,241 --> 00:01:33,240
every up-to-date amazing gadget for his personal use.

21
00:01:34,620 --> 00:01:36,840
Though these wonders of high-tech innovation

22
00:01:36,841 --> 00:01:40,560
often seem to push the envelope of credibility,

23
00:01:40,561 --> 00:01:44,260
any good Bond fan knows the gadgets used by Her Majesty's

24
00:01:44,261 --> 00:01:48,460
secret agent are often based more in fact than fiction.

25
00:01:48,461 --> 00:01:53,460
Most of the vehicles or gadgets you see in a James Bond film,

26
00:01:53,461 --> 00:01:56,460
automatically you'll think, oh, that's ridiculous, that can't be real.

27
00:01:56,461 --> 00:02:00,460
But actually, the case is, they are created for the film,

28
00:02:00,461 --> 00:02:05,460
or they have been something that may be about to appear on the market commercially,

29
00:02:05,461 --> 00:02:07,460
and it's seen in the Bond film for the first time.

30
00:02:07,461 --> 00:02:10,460
So these things, no matter how fantastic they are,

31
00:02:10,461 --> 00:02:13,460
they're based on reality, and they do actually work.

32
00:02:13,461 --> 00:02:16,460
For those who create the gadgets and stunts

33
00:02:16,461 --> 00:02:19,280
and stunts that are part of telling a Bond story,

34
00:02:19,740 --> 00:02:22,900
the experience is nothing less than extraordinary.

35
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:25,760
You're all charged up.

36
00:02:26,480 --> 00:02:29,580
There's never any doubt when somebody asks you

37
00:02:29,581 --> 00:02:30,440
if you can do something.

38
00:02:30,740 --> 00:02:31,660
You know, it's done.

39
00:02:32,080 --> 00:02:32,780
You do it.

40
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:34,800
Something breaks, you fix it.

41
00:02:34,880 --> 00:02:36,380
It doesn't matter if you have to work all night.

42
00:02:37,200 --> 00:02:39,460
When you have done a James Bond movie,

43
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,480
and you yourself are the stunt director

44
00:02:42,481 --> 00:02:43,920
or the stunt coordinator,

45
00:02:43,921 --> 00:02:50,920
Anything else falls short of what a young kid 10 years old

46
00:02:51,020 --> 00:02:54,460
had a dream of at one time in his life,

47
00:02:54,560 --> 00:02:56,960
to do something with cars and automobiles.

48
00:03:01,340 --> 00:03:04,280
80 miles northwest of Phoenix,

49
00:03:04,380 --> 00:03:08,080
parked inside an airplane hangar in the Arizona desert,

50
00:03:08,180 --> 00:03:12,120
is one of James Bond's most memorable gadgets.

51
00:03:12,121 --> 00:03:15,120
It's called the Acrastar Mini Jet,

52
00:03:15,121 --> 00:03:19,120
a one-man, 300-mile-per-hour joyride machine

53
00:03:19,121 --> 00:03:23,120
that wowed audiences in the James Bond film Octopussy.

54
00:03:25,120 --> 00:03:29,120
Airshow pilot Bob Bishop and his wife Mary Ellen

55
00:03:29,121 --> 00:03:31,120
built the jet more than 25 years ago

56
00:03:31,121 --> 00:03:33,120
to be part of his stunt routine.

57
00:03:37,120 --> 00:03:39,120
Today, he puts the jet through its paces

58
00:03:39,121 --> 00:03:41,120
at the airpark where he lives.

59
00:03:42,120 --> 00:03:48,840
We actually are in Arizona partly because we can fly almost 365 days a year.

60
00:03:48,841 --> 00:03:55,720
It's hot in the summertime, you sweat a lot, but in general we have very, very good weather.

61
00:03:55,721 --> 00:04:04,240
When he built the jet, he had no idea it would become famous for being piloted by Bond.

62
00:04:04,241 --> 00:04:11,600
The airplane's fully aerobatic and we can do all types of loops and rolls, fly upside

63
00:04:11,601 --> 00:04:13,640
down, and even have maneuvers in air shows we do,

64
00:04:13,641 --> 00:04:15,840
where we stop the airplane and back it up in the air.

65
00:04:15,841 --> 00:04:18,180
Just go straight up in the air like a world's largest

66
00:04:18,181 --> 00:04:18,881
long dart.

67
00:04:18,882 --> 00:04:19,940
You just throw it straight in the air,

68
00:04:19,941 --> 00:04:22,720
it stops and flips over and comes back down again.

69
00:04:22,721 --> 00:04:25,980
You don't see a lot of jet airplanes doing that.

70
00:04:25,981 --> 00:04:28,380
Bishop and pilot Corky Fornoff

71
00:04:28,381 --> 00:04:30,920
perfected the maneuvers that would become part

72
00:04:30,921 --> 00:04:33,580
of movie-making history.

73
00:04:33,581 --> 00:04:37,260
The little airplane is a perfect match for James Bond.

74
00:04:37,261 --> 00:04:39,960
Because of its size, you could put it in a trailer.

75
00:04:39,961 --> 00:04:43,760
You could have actually dumped it out the back of an airplane and let it fly away.

76
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:46,740
It's really James Bond material.

77
00:04:46,741 --> 00:04:48,220
He can land on a highway.

78
00:04:48,580 --> 00:04:50,700
He can put it in a garage to hide it.

79
00:04:51,120 --> 00:04:53,340
He can almost pull it out of his coat and put it together.

80
00:04:54,380 --> 00:04:57,080
The Bond producers had heard about the jet

81
00:04:57,081 --> 00:05:00,160
and wanted to develop a sequence for the opening of the film.

82
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:04,540
Producer Cubby Broccoli wanted something spectacular,

83
00:05:05,060 --> 00:05:06,980
but didn't want anyone getting hurt.

84
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:09,300
Cubby Broccoli told me one time,

85
00:05:09,301 --> 00:05:11,340
Corky, we aren't hiring you to kill yourself.

86
00:05:11,640 --> 00:05:14,020
We're hiring you to eliminate the risks

87
00:05:14,021 --> 00:05:15,860
and know that you can do it.

88
00:05:19,820 --> 00:05:22,300
Fornhoff and Bishop came up with the idea

89
00:05:22,301 --> 00:05:25,040
of flying the jet through an airplane hangar

90
00:05:25,041 --> 00:05:27,200
to avoid being hit by a missile.

91
00:05:28,300 --> 00:05:29,980
As he flies through this hangar,

92
00:05:30,300 --> 00:05:33,280
hopefully the missile will attach itself to a larger aircraft.

93
00:05:33,900 --> 00:05:35,280
Mr. Bond flies through the hangar.

94
00:05:35,640 --> 00:05:37,540
The missile is attracted to a larger aircraft.

95
00:05:37,860 --> 00:05:39,180
There's one hell of an explosion.

96
00:05:39,300 --> 00:05:42,300
Bond comes out the other side and does a vertical roll.

97
00:05:42,301 --> 00:05:43,300
Classical James Bond.

98
00:05:46,300 --> 00:05:51,300
The amazing thing about the Acrojet was it was a real piece of equipment,

99
00:05:51,301 --> 00:05:54,300
but the Bond people had to do something special with it.

100
00:05:54,301 --> 00:05:57,300
It couldn't just be seen flying through the skies or what have you.

101
00:05:57,301 --> 00:06:01,300
So to shoot the sequence where it goes through the hangar and it's being chased by a missile,

102
00:06:01,301 --> 00:06:06,300
it was a case of taking something real and adding that Bond element to it.

103
00:06:06,301 --> 00:06:08,140
Okay, we're gonna slow it back down, come back down.

104
00:06:08,220 --> 00:06:09,220
Now we're about a mile out.

105
00:06:09,900 --> 00:06:12,780
The Acrostar was so good at doing its stunts

106
00:06:12,781 --> 00:06:15,120
that one scene had to be cut from the film

107
00:06:15,121 --> 00:06:18,100
because it made test audiences too queasy.

108
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:23,980
The scene had the jet disappearing from view down a cliff.

109
00:06:25,920 --> 00:06:27,280
The footage was great.

110
00:06:27,860 --> 00:06:30,360
The problem was when we showed it to the test audience

111
00:06:30,361 --> 00:06:31,820
and watched it in dailies,

112
00:06:32,280 --> 00:06:34,880
as Clay and I came across the top and I pushed away,

113
00:06:34,881 --> 00:06:37,420
It gave the appearance that the whole audience

114
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:39,320
would be sucked into the screen.

115
00:06:39,420 --> 00:06:41,480
And when we first showed it, everybody went,

116
00:06:41,580 --> 00:06:43,520
when we did it.

117
00:06:43,620 --> 00:06:45,360
And we thought it would stay in.

118
00:06:45,460 --> 00:06:47,920
Unfortunately, they decided against it,

119
00:06:48,020 --> 00:06:49,800
because it was a queasy feeling.

120
00:06:49,900 --> 00:06:52,560
Really, it was an unusual illusion.

121
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:56,300
There are actually two Acrastar jets.

122
00:06:56,400 --> 00:06:59,040
Bishop keeps one in his home in Arizona.

123
00:06:59,140 --> 00:07:02,380
And the other, the one that actually flew in the bomb film,

124
00:07:02,381 --> 00:07:07,380
is kept at a flying museum in Los Angeles.

125
00:07:07,480 --> 00:07:10,620
The jet on display no longer has its engine

126
00:07:10,720 --> 00:07:14,020
and no longer flies.

127
00:07:14,120 --> 00:07:17,060
But Bishop's jet is fully operational

128
00:07:17,160 --> 00:07:20,600
and still wows audiences worldwide.

129
00:07:20,700 --> 00:07:22,200
People ask me if this is a toy or not.

130
00:07:22,300 --> 00:07:24,500
Or actually, people think it is not a real airplane.

131
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:26,300
When they walk up to it, they say, where's the real one?

132
00:07:26,400 --> 00:07:28,340
They think this is a model of the airplane.

133
00:07:28,440 --> 00:07:30,580
And they realize it is a real airplane.

134
00:07:30,581 --> 00:07:32,280
the first question I hear usually is,

135
00:07:32,281 --> 00:07:34,580
well, who's the idiot that flies this?

136
00:07:34,581 --> 00:07:35,880
Or the midget, either one.

137
00:07:35,881 --> 00:07:38,780
And I have to raise my hand to both of those.

138
00:07:38,781 --> 00:07:41,320
But I would say this is my work tool, quite frankly.

139
00:07:41,321 --> 00:07:44,420
This is the most one I've ever had flying.

140
00:07:44,421 --> 00:07:48,360
The AcroStar jet has a wingspan of 17 feet.

141
00:07:48,361 --> 00:07:51,260
It's 12 feet long and weighs 430 pounds

142
00:07:51,261 --> 00:07:54,740
without any fuel in its tanks.

143
00:07:54,741 --> 00:08:00,040
A TRS-18 engine made by MicroTurbo powers the plane.

144
00:08:00,041 --> 00:08:07,860
The engine is 12 inches in diameter, 24 inches long, and produces 240 pounds of thrust.

145
00:08:10,640 --> 00:08:17,400
The plane can fly up to 325 miles per hour and can reach altitudes of up to 30,000 feet.

146
00:08:18,160 --> 00:08:20,900
It can fly for two hours before refueling.

147
00:08:22,100 --> 00:08:24,940
I would hate to fly anything smaller than this airplane.

148
00:08:24,941 --> 00:08:28,180
Most cars are longer than its wingspan is,

149
00:08:28,280 --> 00:08:30,820
and most engines and automobiles way more

150
00:08:30,920 --> 00:08:33,420
than this whole airplane together.

151
00:08:33,520 --> 00:08:36,320
When Bishop isn't flying the jet in air shows,

152
00:08:36,420 --> 00:08:39,280
he's been contracted by the US military

153
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:43,460
to use the plane for target practice.

154
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:47,060
Because the Acrostar is so small and so fast,

155
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:50,660
it's actually used by the military to double for a missile

156
00:08:50,760 --> 00:08:53,460
in war games with the Air Force.

157
00:08:53,461 --> 00:08:58,460
though Bishop says the jet is extremely safe he's had to bail out of the plane

158
00:08:58,461 --> 00:09:05,340
twice in the last 25 years both times safely oh I always wear a parachute I'm

159
00:09:05,341 --> 00:09:11,740
crazy not stupid there is a difference though the plane was only featured once

160
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:16,840
in a James Bond film both Bishop and Fornoff say the notoriety of being

161
00:09:16,841 --> 00:09:22,640
associated with 007 and the Acra star left an indelible impact on their lives

162
00:09:22,641 --> 00:09:25,140
and careers.

163
00:09:25,240 --> 00:09:27,840
Through my years of aviation and aviation film work,

164
00:09:27,940 --> 00:09:29,640
I have been given many compliments.

165
00:09:29,740 --> 00:09:31,280
But I think the one I treasure the most

166
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:32,880
came from Cubby Broccoli.

167
00:09:32,980 --> 00:09:34,880
We were at a lunch meeting one day,

168
00:09:34,980 --> 00:09:37,860
and he introduced me to some friends from the Coast Guard.

169
00:09:37,960 --> 00:09:39,760
And he said, this is Corky Fornoff.

170
00:09:39,860 --> 00:09:42,900
He is James Bond's pilot.

171
00:09:43,000 --> 00:09:45,800
When we continue, the makers of James Bond gadgets

172
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:51,000
take 007 from a high-speed acrobatic chase across the skies

173
00:09:51,001 --> 00:09:54,080
to a pitched battle in the deep blue sea.

174
00:09:58,240 --> 00:10:00,040
In the film Octopussy,

175
00:10:00,200 --> 00:10:03,420
James Bond must transport the Aquastar jet on the ground

176
00:10:03,421 --> 00:10:04,680
without being noticed.

177
00:10:05,640 --> 00:10:09,020
He winds up towing the tiny plane inside a horse trailer.

178
00:10:10,100 --> 00:10:12,500
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

179
00:10:16,360 --> 00:10:19,420
Inside a work shed in central Florida,

180
00:10:19,421 --> 00:10:23,120
Oscar-winning underwater cinematographer Jordan Klein

181
00:10:23,121 --> 00:10:25,920
keeps one of his favorite cinema secrets.

182
00:10:25,921 --> 00:10:29,420
It's one of only two remaining toe sleds

183
00:10:29,421 --> 00:10:31,760
used in the classic underwater fight scene

184
00:10:31,761 --> 00:10:34,460
in the James Bond film Thunderball.

185
00:10:37,380 --> 00:10:40,380
This is the first time Klein and his son Jordy,

186
00:10:40,381 --> 00:10:42,380
also an underwater cameraman,

187
00:10:42,381 --> 00:10:45,420
have taken the sled down in 10 years.

188
00:10:49,420 --> 00:10:56,420
They load the rig onto a specially designed film boat and head for Rainbow Springs,

189
00:10:56,421 --> 00:11:04,420
a crystal clear river half an hour from their home.

190
00:11:04,421 --> 00:11:11,420
Here, central Floridians cool off from the oppressive summer heat and humidity, while

191
00:11:11,421 --> 00:11:18,420
Jordan and his crew look for the right spot to demonstrate how James Bond magic was made.

192
00:11:18,421 --> 00:11:30,360
The sled, which was designed by Klein for the film, is lowered into the water much the way it was nearly 30 years ago off the coast of Nassau.

193
00:11:31,300 --> 00:11:41,360
Back then, Klein worked with 40 divers, a score of cameramen, a dozen tow sleds and a Vulcan bomber to capture Bond's most famous underwater sequence.

194
00:11:41,361 --> 00:11:42,061
sequence.

195
00:11:42,062 --> 00:11:48,560
Normally we expect people swimming scuba divers or what have you but being a Bond film

196
00:11:48,561 --> 00:11:52,960
we've got to have something different and Jordan Klein came up with the idea of these toe sleds as

197
00:11:52,961 --> 00:11:57,840
they were named and so we had the frogman attached them to the back it had a motor and it took them

198
00:11:57,841 --> 00:12:02,080
to the water at a fast speed but being a Bond film still has to be something different so it had

199
00:12:02,081 --> 00:12:07,840
harpoon guns fitted to the front so it was a nice gadget which is futuristic in its day and probably

200
00:12:07,841 --> 00:12:09,849
never been copied to this day.

201
00:12:09,850 --> 00:12:14,000
In these rare behind-the-scenes home movies of the film shoot,

202
00:12:14,001 --> 00:12:20,400
the achingly slow process of making an action film sequence underwater is more than evident.

203
00:12:24,800 --> 00:12:28,160
We'd go out and put all the equipment in the water in the morning. In one instance,

204
00:12:28,161 --> 00:12:33,360
we started at 8 o'clock in the morning and we changed tanks underwater and myself and

205
00:12:33,361 --> 00:12:36,300
and another person didn't come up until after 6,

206
00:12:36,301 --> 00:12:39,100
changing tanks underwater and shooting, changing cameras,

207
00:12:39,101 --> 00:12:40,500
changing film.

208
00:12:40,501 --> 00:12:43,360
And at that point, I was running the bomb carrier.

209
00:12:43,361 --> 00:12:44,700
So I just stayed there.

210
00:12:44,701 --> 00:12:46,600
So it was arduous.

211
00:12:46,601 --> 00:12:49,340
And when you got up to the surface in the evening,

212
00:12:49,341 --> 00:12:50,720
you were pretty bad off.

213
00:12:50,721 --> 00:12:53,120
A couple of times, I got violently sick.

214
00:12:53,121 --> 00:12:55,240
And they thought I wasn't going to be work the next day.

215
00:12:55,241 --> 00:12:57,880
And 4 o'clock in the morning, they pound on your door

216
00:12:57,881 --> 00:12:59,080
and say, come on, let's go.

217
00:12:59,081 --> 00:13:01,060
So you go.

218
00:13:01,061 --> 00:13:04,960
Despite its age, Jordan Klein Sr. says everything

219
00:13:05,060 --> 00:13:08,340
about the sleds were designed to reflect the super secret,

220
00:13:08,440 --> 00:13:12,800
sometimes violent world of James Bond.

221
00:13:12,900 --> 00:13:17,180
The scooter has two headlights, two spear guns powered by CO2

222
00:13:17,280 --> 00:13:21,480
cartridges, and two horsepower plus electric motor.

223
00:13:21,580 --> 00:13:24,320
And it was designed for pulling stuff, which it did.

224
00:13:24,420 --> 00:13:27,180
It pulled the cargo net over the Vulcan bomber.

225
00:13:27,280 --> 00:13:29,960
And the cargo net was a pretty hefty piece of equipment.

226
00:13:29,961 --> 00:13:31,560
Divers could just barely pick it up.

227
00:13:32,180 --> 00:13:34,900
So it took six of them to pick it up and move it across the bottom

228
00:13:34,901 --> 00:13:37,020
and then ultimately across the Vulcan bomber.

229
00:13:38,300 --> 00:13:41,920
It took the Nassau crew three months to gather enough footage

230
00:13:41,921 --> 00:13:45,580
for the massive 20-minute battle sequence that wound up in the film.

231
00:13:47,440 --> 00:13:50,260
The tow sleds weren't based on anything in reality.

232
00:13:50,680 --> 00:13:52,360
This was something created for the film.

233
00:13:52,900 --> 00:13:56,540
And I think it was just taking an underwater mode one step further.

234
00:13:56,541 --> 00:14:00,540
Maybe today in exploration, they've got variations on the theme.

235
00:14:00,640 --> 00:14:03,540
But again, in a Bond film, they have to instigate things.

236
00:14:03,640 --> 00:14:05,540
So you've seen something you hadn't seen before.

237
00:14:05,640 --> 00:14:08,540
You watch the film and it adds that extra attraction.

238
00:14:08,640 --> 00:14:10,540
Oh, wow, what's that? It's another gadget,

239
00:14:10,640 --> 00:14:12,540
something we've not seen before.

240
00:14:12,640 --> 00:14:14,540
Totally realistic. It totally works.

241
00:14:14,640 --> 00:14:16,540
And again, Bond did it first.

242
00:14:16,640 --> 00:14:19,540
Jordan still remembers the moment

243
00:14:19,640 --> 00:14:22,540
when the final frame of film was in the can.

244
00:14:22,541 --> 00:14:26,540
We worked until about four in the morning and finished.

245
00:14:26,541 --> 00:14:30,540
And I've got a picture on the wall here of everybody standing in the bar

246
00:14:30,541 --> 00:14:34,540
at Staniel Key right after we finished Thunderball.

247
00:14:34,541 --> 00:14:36,540
And that was the last scene.

248
00:14:36,541 --> 00:14:39,540
You didn't know whether you want to just fall down on the floor and go to sleep

249
00:14:39,541 --> 00:14:42,540
or whether you want to jump up and down with joy because it was over.

250
00:14:42,541 --> 00:14:46,540
But really, it was sort of a sad feeling because there was no tomorrow.

251
00:14:46,541 --> 00:14:50,540
There was no, we're going to get going again and do something.

252
00:14:50,541 --> 00:14:52,040
and do something, we're gonna do this scene,

253
00:14:52,041 --> 00:14:53,940
that scene tomorrow, it was over.

254
00:14:53,941 --> 00:14:56,180
It was absolutely over and it was a big letdown

255
00:14:56,181 --> 00:14:57,380
at that point for me.

256
00:15:00,720 --> 00:15:02,580
On the Rainbow Springs River,

257
00:15:02,581 --> 00:15:05,280
Jordan and his son worked to perfect the motions

258
00:15:05,281 --> 00:15:07,520
done in the movie decades ago.

259
00:15:08,720 --> 00:15:11,600
Special underwater camera housings are assembled

260
00:15:11,601 --> 00:15:14,400
so the Klein film team can capture the action

261
00:15:14,401 --> 00:15:15,740
beneath the surface.

262
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:18,460
Can you find a little bit of deep auger for this bass?

263
00:15:18,461 --> 00:15:27,480
The sled slowly slips beneath the surface for the first time since it was used in the

264
00:15:27,481 --> 00:15:29,480
Bond film more than 30 years ago.

265
00:15:31,240 --> 00:15:34,020
Many of the original sleds were motorized.

266
00:15:34,220 --> 00:15:35,300
A few were towed.

267
00:15:35,800 --> 00:15:38,400
This sled had its motor removed years ago.

268
00:15:39,500 --> 00:15:44,540
Today, a motorboat pulling a thin line of monofilament will move this sled through the

269
00:15:44,541 --> 00:15:45,241
water.

270
00:15:48,460 --> 00:15:52,460
But on one of its first runs, the sled shows its age.

271
00:15:52,461 --> 00:15:58,460
The pressure from the water flying past, even at just five knots, rips off a piece of the housing.

272
00:16:01,460 --> 00:16:06,460
The Kleins are forced to make repairs in the water to get the sled up and running again.

273
00:16:08,460 --> 00:16:12,460
When it was up like this, there was perforated holes in the bottom,

274
00:16:12,461 --> 00:16:15,460
and the pressure of the water going into those holes

275
00:16:15,461 --> 00:16:19,260
ripped all the screws off of the top plate of the sled here.

276
00:16:19,261 --> 00:16:21,960
So all of a sudden the whole top blew off of it.

277
00:16:21,961 --> 00:16:25,460
So we had to scramble and find a bunch of nuts and bolts

278
00:16:25,461 --> 00:16:27,460
so that they wouldn't rip out anymore.

279
00:16:33,460 --> 00:16:37,460
After a few test runs, the Klein team finally gets the hang

280
00:16:37,461 --> 00:16:41,460
of pulling the sled through the crystal clear spring water.

281
00:16:41,461 --> 00:16:42,400
A little faster.

282
00:16:43,900 --> 00:16:49,940
The wobbly start reminded Klein of what it was like the first time he tested the sled off the Florida coast.

283
00:16:51,760 --> 00:16:58,200
The first time I put this in the water, jumped in the water, face mask and pair of shorts, and punched it.

284
00:16:58,880 --> 00:17:02,780
And the face mask ended up down around my neck, and my bathing suit was down around my ankles.

285
00:17:03,700 --> 00:17:09,880
So needless to say, as an engineer, I came to the conclusion that I should reduce the power a little bit,

286
00:17:09,881 --> 00:17:14,320
which we did, and obviously everything worked out pretty well during the production.

287
00:17:15,860 --> 00:17:22,460
After a half an hour, Klein is able to guide the sled much like he did during the production of Thunderball.

288
00:17:23,920 --> 00:17:26,860
Controlling the sled is simple. It's all body movement.

289
00:17:27,240 --> 00:17:29,760
Push down on the control bars and the sled moves up.

290
00:17:30,180 --> 00:17:32,200
Push up and the sled noses down.

291
00:17:34,360 --> 00:17:37,900
Right and left are determined by the position of the driver's body,

292
00:17:37,901 --> 00:17:43,600
but keeping up with the underwater filming is not as easy as the Klein team makes it look.

293
00:17:44,700 --> 00:17:49,660
People don't realize that even though it looks like it's being towed relatively slow,

294
00:17:50,280 --> 00:17:54,300
to swim that speed, even though this thing was probably only being towed about five knots,

295
00:17:54,680 --> 00:17:57,980
the average person can only swim about two to three knots.

296
00:17:58,500 --> 00:18:01,240
Fortunately, we had two camera people down there.

297
00:18:01,680 --> 00:18:04,580
I would only be able to keep up with him for a certain amount of time,

298
00:18:04,660 --> 00:18:06,140
and then he would blow past me.

299
00:18:06,141 --> 00:18:13,140
Well, we were flying around and crashing once in a while, unfortunately.

300
00:18:13,141 --> 00:18:18,900
Just shallow spots and deep spots, and the banking turns are tough with this rig because

301
00:18:18,901 --> 00:18:20,620
it doesn't like to do any of that.

302
00:18:20,621 --> 00:18:25,260
It likes to fly flat, and if you're going too fast, it'll dive, and if you're going too

303
00:18:25,261 --> 00:18:27,260
slow, it wants to go to the surface.

304
00:18:27,261 --> 00:18:29,260
But it was great.

305
00:18:29,261 --> 00:18:37,720
Sort of reminded me a great deal of the actual happening.

306
00:18:37,721 --> 00:18:41,760
When we were over in the Bahamas, eight, ten scooters whizzing around, chasing at each

307
00:18:41,761 --> 00:18:42,760
other.

308
00:18:42,761 --> 00:18:45,920
And then during the fight scene, of course, there was a whole pile of people, 40-some

309
00:18:45,921 --> 00:18:51,220
people in the scene, and they were whizzing around, shooting spears and trying to kill

310
00:18:51,221 --> 00:18:54,120
each other.

311
00:18:54,121 --> 00:18:58,760
After 45 minutes in the water, it's time to pull the sled out and set sail for the

312
00:18:58,761 --> 00:19:04,820
Klein Hall. For Jordan Klein Sr., the experience showed him his Bond gadget

313
00:19:04,821 --> 00:19:12,320
still works. It also brought back memories from when he first used the sled

314
00:19:12,321 --> 00:19:17,780
and thoughts of his son who was just one year old when Thunderball was made.

315
00:19:17,781 --> 00:19:22,820
I looked at the scooter and I looked at him so that sort of brought it into a

316
00:19:22,821 --> 00:19:27,980
sharp perspective and it was great it was a great feeling. He became part of

317
00:19:27,981 --> 00:19:30,340
a thunder ball for a short period of time.

318
00:19:32,220 --> 00:19:35,260
Up next, an American stunt promoter

319
00:19:35,261 --> 00:19:37,560
captures the attention of Bond filmmakers

320
00:19:37,561 --> 00:19:40,900
and winds up creating movie-making history.

321
00:19:43,720 --> 00:19:47,140
When Sean Connery left the role of James Bond

322
00:19:47,141 --> 00:19:50,840
after Diamonds Are Forever, he said, never again.

323
00:19:51,480 --> 00:19:54,620
Years later, after being convinced to make another Bond movie,

324
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:57,820
his wife gave the new film its title, Never.

325
00:19:57,980 --> 00:19:59,280
Say never again.

326
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:01,620
Modern Marvels will return.

327
00:20:04,500 --> 00:20:07,760
Jay Milligan knows his way around race cars.

328
00:20:08,380 --> 00:20:10,300
He's been working with them all his life.

329
00:20:11,520 --> 00:20:14,400
In more than four decades of working with cars,

330
00:20:14,900 --> 00:20:18,000
the Buffalo native turned his passion for automobiles

331
00:20:18,001 --> 00:20:20,240
into a full-time professional.

332
00:20:20,241 --> 00:20:30,100
He has a successful business promoting auto races and demolition derbies in the Northeast.

333
00:20:31,300 --> 00:20:37,640
But he is perhaps best known as the mind behind one of the greatest car stunts in filmmaking history,

334
00:20:38,420 --> 00:20:43,800
the 360-degree barrel roll done in the film The Man with the Golden Gun.

335
00:20:43,801 --> 00:20:50,800
He still drives the backup car used for that daring stunt.

336
00:20:50,801 --> 00:20:53,800
While the original stays safe in a museum,

337
00:20:53,801 --> 00:20:56,800
the old AMC Hornet can still cut corners

338
00:20:56,801 --> 00:20:59,800
like it did on the Bond set.

339
00:20:59,801 --> 00:21:03,800
I was always interested in anything with wheels

340
00:21:03,801 --> 00:21:08,800
from a childhood of toys and cars and wagons and scooters.

341
00:21:08,801 --> 00:21:11,800
My whole life as a former race car driver,

342
00:21:11,801 --> 00:21:20,360
car driver the dream never came to me that we would ever succeed in handling and being the stunt

343
00:21:20,361 --> 00:21:26,360
director of a james bond movie that was the furthest thing from my mind milligan's saga to

344
00:21:26,361 --> 00:21:33,720
reach the silver screen began in the 1960s when he was running his stunt program called the american

345
00:21:33,721 --> 00:21:40,440
thrill show the shows which were a favorite at county fairs were done with the cooperation of

346
00:21:40,441 --> 00:21:44,340
of the old American Motors Corporation, AMC,

347
00:21:44,341 --> 00:21:46,980
and Milligan used AMC cars in the show.

348
00:21:48,240 --> 00:21:52,840
At the same time, engineers at Cornell Aeronautical Laboratory

349
00:21:52,841 --> 00:21:57,640
were studying how cars behaved in rollover accidents.

350
00:21:57,641 --> 00:21:59,540
The engineers contacted Milligan

351
00:21:59,541 --> 00:22:01,820
with an interesting proposal.

352
00:22:01,821 --> 00:22:04,360
Their studies had shown they could make a car

353
00:22:04,361 --> 00:22:09,360
do a 360-degree barrel roll and land on its wheels unscathed.

354
00:22:09,361 --> 00:22:12,000
The local newspapers picked up the story,

355
00:22:12,100 --> 00:22:14,360
and representatives of the Houston Astrodome

356
00:22:14,460 --> 00:22:16,400
contacted Milligan.

357
00:22:16,500 --> 00:22:19,000
If there was going to be an attempt at this stunt,

358
00:22:19,100 --> 00:22:23,440
they wanted it done at their facility first.

359
00:22:23,540 --> 00:22:26,640
Somehow, Walter Cronkite heard about it.

360
00:22:26,740 --> 00:22:31,020
And on CBS News, he said, this week,

361
00:22:31,120 --> 00:22:34,060
an extravaganza is taking place in the Astrodome,

362
00:22:34,160 --> 00:22:36,680
where a car is going to roll over ramp to ramp

363
00:22:36,780 --> 00:22:38,720
and land and drive away.

364
00:22:38,721 --> 00:22:41,100
The ticket sales went bonanza.

365
00:22:41,960 --> 00:22:48,300
We sold the Astrodome out in the two nights, 98,602 paid admissions.

366
00:22:49,960 --> 00:22:56,620
The reason why the delicate stunt was so difficult was because the numbers had to be exact.

367
00:22:57,160 --> 00:23:02,840
The slightest miscalculation by the driver and the stunt could quickly turn to disaster.

368
00:23:02,841 --> 00:23:09,160
The numbers that most important is line up plus or minus two inches to the left or the right.

369
00:23:10,180 --> 00:23:17,600
The second important thing is the speed, and then anything under 39 and a half or over 40 and a half miles an hour

370
00:23:17,601 --> 00:23:25,380
would lead to a second roll over on the landing ramp or crashing into the landing ramp if you're too slow.

371
00:23:26,340 --> 00:23:30,960
The car achieves a trajectory at its very height of about 22 feet.

372
00:23:30,961 --> 00:23:36,640
It does a snap roll considered to be close to two and a half G's.

373
00:23:36,641 --> 00:23:42,640
And the things that are very important is pitch, yaw, and roll velocity.

374
00:23:42,641 --> 00:23:47,840
All engineering factors that had to be put into the takeoff ramp design

375
00:23:47,841 --> 00:23:53,440
to make sure that the car can achieve the pitch and the roll velocity.

376
00:23:53,441 --> 00:24:00,200
To achieve enough speed, the AMC Hornet used for the stunt had to exit the stadium

377
00:24:00,201 --> 00:24:02,800
to get a running start.

378
00:24:02,801 --> 00:24:07,600
The driver made several passes to get a feel for the ramps.

379
00:24:07,601 --> 00:24:12,980
After about the third pass, 45,000 Texans stood up

380
00:24:12,981 --> 00:24:16,780
and started jeering the driver and stamping their feet.

381
00:24:20,220 --> 00:24:23,700
The driver on the next pass went over the ramp,

382
00:24:23,701 --> 00:24:25,820
landed successfully, drove around,

383
00:24:25,821 --> 00:24:31,660
and the Astrodome people and the 40-some thousand people there just went crazy.

384
00:24:32,620 --> 00:24:36,500
The event became an immediate hit on the car show circuit,

385
00:24:36,880 --> 00:24:41,440
but Milligan had no idea just how high his car stunt would take him.

386
00:24:43,880 --> 00:24:49,360
Several weeks later, while doing a show in Pennsylvania, Milligan's hotel phone ran.

387
00:24:49,880 --> 00:24:53,260
I was sitting in a hotel room in Hershey, Pennsylvania,

388
00:24:53,261 --> 00:24:56,600
And about 9 o'clock in the morning, I got a phone call,

389
00:24:56,780 --> 00:24:59,700
and the gentleman said, this is Cubby Broccoli.

390
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:01,400
I'm with James Bond.

391
00:25:01,940 --> 00:25:04,780
Could you perform this stunt in a James Bond movie?

392
00:25:05,800 --> 00:25:09,900
Broccoli had questions about the speed and ramp space needed for the cars.

393
00:25:10,780 --> 00:25:14,220
Then he told Milligan where he was going to perform the stunt.

394
00:25:15,280 --> 00:25:18,660
And he said, well, we're going to be doing this in Bangkok, Thailand.

395
00:25:19,280 --> 00:25:21,740
We would like to start shooting in March.

396
00:25:21,741 --> 00:25:25,900
can you have two cars built and shipped?

397
00:25:27,040 --> 00:25:29,420
They paved a quarter of a mile roadway

398
00:25:29,421 --> 00:25:33,480
that was not a road, it was in a field for the approach.

399
00:25:33,960 --> 00:25:37,060
And the ramps were built on both sides of the Klong,

400
00:25:37,280 --> 00:25:38,960
that's a waterway in Thailand.

401
00:25:39,640 --> 00:25:41,700
They built the ramps out of teakwood.

402
00:25:42,560 --> 00:25:44,820
We had shipped 14 cars over there,

403
00:25:45,040 --> 00:25:47,680
some were police cars, some were backup cars

404
00:25:47,681 --> 00:25:50,660
for the spiral stunt, the James Bond car itself.

405
00:25:50,661 --> 00:25:57,260
and we had camera people mounting camera mounts in the cars on the outside of the cars for all

406
00:25:57,261 --> 00:25:59,660
the chase scenes in the movie.

407
00:25:59,661 --> 00:26:04,460
Milligan drove the Hornet in many of the chase scenes filmed

408
00:26:04,461 --> 00:26:08,532
around Bangkok in a way that Milligan could not believe.

409
00:26:08,533 --> 00:26:11,440
A lot of people don't understand that in

410
00:26:11,441 --> 00:26:16,116
Bangkok you can't stop the world for a movie shoot.

411
00:26:16,117 --> 00:26:19,600
It's very crowded with bicycles and an

412
00:26:19,601 --> 00:26:21,640
awful lot of automobiles.

413
00:26:21,641 --> 00:26:25,140
And we did all the chase scenes in normal traffic.

414
00:26:25,141 --> 00:26:28,880
We weaved in and out, cut them off, went up on the grass.

415
00:26:28,881 --> 00:26:33,680
And there was a director of our movie who was a Thai national,

416
00:26:33,681 --> 00:26:34,940
lived in Bangkok.

417
00:26:34,941 --> 00:26:39,620
And he would come up behind us and pay for any damage

418
00:26:39,621 --> 00:26:43,380
or any repairs that ensued in route of the chase scene.

419
00:26:43,381 --> 00:26:46,960
And he was quite busy.

420
00:26:46,961 --> 00:26:51,300
The day of the spiral stunt, Milligan says everyone on set was tense.

421
00:26:52,080 --> 00:26:54,740
The crew had one shot to get the stunt right.

422
00:26:55,520 --> 00:26:59,500
Doing it more than once would be time-consuming and costly.

423
00:27:00,660 --> 00:27:07,340
We were given a one-hour notice by Guy Hamilton, the director of the movie, to be ready to go.

424
00:27:07,341 --> 00:27:08,960
We had the driver suited up.

425
00:27:09,100 --> 00:27:13,940
We had Madikens in the car, one representing Roger Moore as James Bond,

426
00:27:13,941 --> 00:27:17,580
and the other Clifton James as the Southern Sheriff Pepper.

427
00:27:17,920 --> 00:27:21,660
And we got down to the countdown of 10 minutes, 5 minutes.

428
00:27:22,160 --> 00:27:24,420
When it got to zero, I heard that car.

429
00:27:24,580 --> 00:27:29,240
I was on the landing side, standing with Roger Moore and Cubby Broccoli.

430
00:27:30,620 --> 00:27:37,020
And the car went up a ramp, did a perfect spiral, landed.

431
00:27:38,340 --> 00:27:40,520
And the driver got off the landing ramp.

432
00:27:40,600 --> 00:27:42,920
He was told to turn left. He turned left.

433
00:27:42,921 --> 00:27:48,920
I can't describe the jubilation, the feeling, the satisfaction...

434
00:27:48,921 --> 00:27:51,920
...of doing something right the first time.

435
00:27:52,920 --> 00:27:55,920
They'd only got one shot at this, it'd never been done before.

436
00:27:55,921 --> 00:27:59,920
OK, they'd tried it on a computer, but this was a guy risking his life.

437
00:27:59,921 --> 00:28:03,920
And when you see that sequence on the film, generally people laugh...

438
00:28:03,921 --> 00:28:05,920
...because they can't believe what they're seeing.

439
00:28:05,921 --> 00:28:08,920
But in reality, that was a really dangerous stunt.

440
00:28:08,921 --> 00:28:11,920
And still to this day, one of the most spectacular ones you've seen in a James Bond film...

441
00:28:11,921 --> 00:28:14,520
Bond film, excellent.

442
00:28:14,521 --> 00:28:18,100
Milligan's crew duplicated the stunt about 30 times

443
00:28:18,101 --> 00:28:20,860
after making the Bond film.

444
00:28:20,861 --> 00:28:24,260
But he's since retired the dangerous move.

445
00:28:24,261 --> 00:28:28,240
He says the legacy of the spiral will live on,

446
00:28:28,241 --> 00:28:30,780
only in the Bond picture.

447
00:28:30,781 --> 00:28:34,580
But the secret of exactly how the stunt is done

448
00:28:34,581 --> 00:28:37,320
will die with him.

449
00:28:37,321 --> 00:28:41,280
There are people simulating it in the movies today,

450
00:28:41,281 --> 00:28:44,180
where the car goes up the ramp, turns to the left,

451
00:28:44,181 --> 00:28:46,080
and crashes on its left side.

452
00:28:46,081 --> 00:28:50,820
But no one has ever been able to duplicate it.

453
00:28:50,821 --> 00:28:52,760
I'd give them credit if they could.

454
00:28:52,761 --> 00:28:55,020
The secret would go with me.

455
00:28:55,021 --> 00:28:57,560
Coming up, the gadgets of James Bond

456
00:28:57,561 --> 00:29:02,200
continue to fascinate the public, 40 years after 007

457
00:29:02,201 --> 00:29:06,980
first graced the silver screen.

458
00:29:06,981 --> 00:29:09,980
To guarantee the safety of the driver in the AMC Hornet

459
00:29:09,981 --> 00:29:12,980
Bond producers had two divers in the river,

460
00:29:12,981 --> 00:29:14,980
as well as a heavy lifting crane

461
00:29:14,981 --> 00:29:17,980
that could quickly lift the car from the murky water.

462
00:29:17,981 --> 00:29:20,980
Modern Marvels will return in a moment.

463
00:29:22,980 --> 00:29:25,980
Among the rolling green fields of the British countryside

464
00:29:25,981 --> 00:29:29,980
in an idyllic land called the New Forest,

465
00:29:29,981 --> 00:29:32,980
the specter of England's most famous secret agent

466
00:29:32,981 --> 00:29:34,980
lingers on the roadway.

467
00:29:34,981 --> 00:29:40,980
The 1974 Hornet from the film The Man with the Golden Gun

468
00:29:40,981 --> 00:29:44,980
motors its way among horses and pine trees.

469
00:29:46,980 --> 00:29:51,980
The car is part of an extensive collection of Bond vehicles and gadgets

470
00:29:51,981 --> 00:29:54,980
at England's National Motor Museum at Bewley.

471
00:29:55,980 --> 00:30:00,980
The museum is caretaker to hundreds of classic British automobiles,

472
00:30:00,981 --> 00:30:05,980
from century-old touring cars to the fastest cars in the world.

473
00:30:06,980 --> 00:30:11,980
The Bond exhibit began in 2001 with vehicles from Eon Productions,

474
00:30:11,981 --> 00:30:14,980
the makers of the Bond films, and Doug Radinius,

475
00:30:14,981 --> 00:30:17,980
vice president of the Ian Fleming Foundation,

476
00:30:17,981 --> 00:30:20,980
which honors the memory of the Bond author.

477
00:30:22,980 --> 00:30:26,980
Radinius has an extensive collection of Bond memorabilia,

478
00:30:26,981 --> 00:30:29,980
gadgets, and vehicles at his home.

479
00:30:29,981 --> 00:30:35,620
One of the more remarkable gadgets is the cell phone that could drive a car by remote

480
00:30:35,621 --> 00:30:36,620
control.

481
